{
  "id": "pachuco_boogie",
  "name": "Pachuco boogie (1940s Los Angeles caló jump)",
  "family": "jazz",
  "lineage": "Mexican-American jump and boogie-woogie of 1940s Los Angeles, sung in caló, the slang of the zoot-suited pachuco youth, over swing-band and jump-blues arrangements. The Don Tosti Band's 'Pachuco Boogie' of 1948, half-spoken in caló over a boogie piano and saxophone groove, became one of the biggest-selling Latin records of its day, and Lalo Guerrero, later called the father of Chicano music, recorded caló boogies and swing numbers in the same years.",
  "recipe": "classic North American pachuco boogie, improv On Frame, boogie woogie branch. saxophone, upright piano, upright bass, bronze bell bronze brushes-and-sticks drum kit, bach stradivarius 180 canonical American orchestral trumpet. dry but alive 1948-1965 indie-commercial early tape us small. passive ribbon, tube pre, German tube console, vari-mu, Pultec passive program EQ. tape 15ips.",
